<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Like Walking in the Afternoon. – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Time for Exercise</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Like Walking in the Afternoon.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Respond to texts with personal reflections. Reading: Participate in group discussions based on reading assignments. Phonemic Awareness: Decode English graphemes and phonemes using knowledge of word parts, syllabification, and phonemic awareness.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can respond to texts about exercise, with personal reflections (e.g., "I think exercising is fun."). Can participate in group discussions about exercise, based on reading assignments (e.g., "What are some simple exercises we can do?" "We can jump rope, ride a bike, or run."). Can decode English graphemes and phonemes by segmenting words into parts, identifying syllables, and applying phonemic awareness (e.g., decoding "wellness" as "well-ness" and "exciting" as "ex-cit-ing").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0e6ea;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; text-align: left;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; text-align: left;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; text-align: left;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a series of flashcards showing different exercises (e.g., a person walking, running, swimming, cycling, dancing). Students identify the activities by reading the word on the card (e.g., "walk," "run," "swim"). The teacher asks, "Do you like to walk in the afternoon?" to connect to the theme and activate prior vocabulary from Lesson 1.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary words from the flashcards and the previous lesson, such as "walk," "swim," "run," "exercise," "exciting," and "healthy," paying attention to the /k/ sound in "can" and the /s/ sound in "swim." The teacher demonstrates simple sentences like "I like walking in the afternoon." and "I can swim."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of "exercise" and "healthy activities."</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is swimming an exercise?" (Yes, it is.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is watching TV an exercise?" (No, it is not.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Time for Exercise"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated narrative text titled "Our Afternoon Activities" about three children and their favorite exercises, using target vocabulary (walk, run, swim, cycle, exciting, friend, every day) and grammar (Present simple, modal verbs 'can').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Text Example:</b><br>"My name is Ana. I like walking in the park every day. Walking is healthy and fun. My friend, Leo, likes to run with his dog. He can run very fast! And my other friend, Maria, likes swimming. She goes to the pool sometimes. We all like to be active in the afternoon!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interprets main ideas and specific details in short, simple texts about familiar topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the text aloud once.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the story about?" (It's about friends and their exercises.)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who likes walking?" (Ana likes walking.)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Statements (Students read and identify):</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Ana likes running." (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Leo can run very fast." (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Maria goes to the gym every day." (False, she goes to the pool sometimes)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension, identifying vocabulary, decoding multisyllabic words, using present simple questions and answers, modal 'can'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: "Fill the Gaps" (Controlled Practice)</b>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students receive a handout with sentences from the "Our Afternoon Activities" text, but with key vocabulary words missing. They read each sentence and choose the correct word from a word bank (e.g., <i>walk, run, swim, healthy, afternoon</i>) to complete it. The teacher monitors and helps with reading difficulties.</p>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "Ana likes to _____ in the park." (walk)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "Walking is _____ and fun." (healthy)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: "Decode and Clap" (Phonemic Awareness)</b>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher writes target multisyllabic words from the text on the board (e.g., "wellness," "exciting," "activity," "afternoon," "exercise"). Students, in pairs, read each word aloud, segment it into syllables by clapping for each syllable, and draw lines to separate them (e.g., "well-ness," "ex-cit-ing"). They practice reading the segmented words.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: "Question Hunt" (Pair Reading and Peer Check)</b>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a worksheet with 3-4 WH-questions about the "Our Afternoon Activities" text (e.g., "What does Ana like to do?", "What type of exercise does Leo like?"). Students re-read the text together to find and underline the answers. They then take turns reading a question and the answer to their partner, checking each other's reading and comprehension.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Time for Exercise"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group reading and discussion leading to personal reflection and suggestions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity: "My Favorite Exercise Idea"</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher prepares several small cards, each with a short description of an exercise (e.g., "Cycling with a friend in the park is active and fun.", "Horseback riding is an exciting sport.", "Dancing to music makes you healthy."). Each card includes one or two target vocabulary words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into small groups. Each group receives a set of 4-5 "exercise idea" cards.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">First, each student in the group takes a turn reading one card aloud to their group members, focusing on clear pronunciation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Then, as a group, they read all the cards again and discuss: "What type of exercise do you like from these cards?" and "Which activity can you do with a friend?" (Pragmatic: Asking/Expressing preferences. Sociolinguistic: Encouraging others).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students respond to the texts with personal reflections (e.g., "I like dancing because it is fun!" or "I think horseback riding is exciting, but I cannot do it."). They also discuss simple exercises they *can* do, like "We can jump rope together!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Finally, each group chooses one activity they all agree is good for friends and prepares to share their choice and reason with the class (e.g., "We like walking because it's healthy and we can talk to our friends.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher observes students during the "My Favorite Exercise Idea" group discussion and when groups share their chosen activity and reasons.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Reads exercise cards intelligibly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., <i>walk, run, swim, exciting, healthy, friend</i>) from the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clearly expresses personal preferences based on the reading.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participates actively and collaboratively in group discussion.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Applies phonemic awareness when reading words (e.g., "ex-cit-ing").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short paper slip before leaving.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can read about different exercises." (Yes / No / A little)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can tell my friends what exercise I like." (Yes / No / A little)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompts:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most interesting part of reading about exercise today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new exercise word did you learn to read or understand better?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The reading activities allowed students to engage with the target vocabulary and grammar in context. The "Decode and Clap" activity was effective in developing phonemic awareness. Students generally responded well to the "Question Hunt" and the "My Favorite Exercise Idea" task, demonstrating comprehension and personal reflection.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Adjustments for next time:</b> Some students struggled with reading longer sentences independently. I will ensure more visual support for difficult words and model reading strategies more explicitly. I might also provide sentence frames for expressing preferences for lower-level readers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The group discussions and personal reflections from today's reading lesson will naturally transition into Lesson 3, which focuses on Speaking & Interaction. Students will use the exercises and preferences they read and discussed today as a basis for direct oral interaction and suggesting activities to peers.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>